1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,140
Aarav builds a circuit with
exactly the right resistor value.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:08,966
A few seconds later, the resistor is
too hot to touch, and it smells burnt.

3
00:00:09,316 --> 00:00:10,773
The value was correct.

4
00:00:10,773 --> 00:00:12,092
So what went wrong?

5
00:00:12,592 --> 00:00:14,624
The value is only one number.

6
00:00:14,624 --> 00:00:18,105
Every part also has limits,
called its specifications.

7
00:00:18,455 --> 00:00:21,996
They are printed in a document
from the maker, called a datasheet.

8
00:00:22,646 --> 00:00:25,058
A datasheet has a few main sections.

9
00:00:25,408 --> 00:00:28,329
First, a short description
and the key features.

10
00:00:28,679 --> 00:00:30,871
Then the absolute maximum ratings.

11
00:00:30,871 --> 00:00:33,376
These are limits you must never cross.

12
00:00:33,726 --> 00:00:38,065
Then the electrical characteristics,
with minimum, typical and maximum

13
00:00:38,065 --> 00:00:38,965
values.

14
00:00:39,083 --> 00:00:43,132
Then graphs, and finally the size
and shape of the package, so you know

15
00:00:43,132 --> 00:00:44,388
it fits your board.

16
00:00:45,038 --> 00:00:46,718
Which numbers matter most?

17
00:00:46,718 --> 00:00:50,902
For a resistor, the resistance,
the tolerance and the power rating.

18
00:00:51,252 --> 00:00:55,493
For a capacitor, the capacitance,
the tolerance and the voltage rating.

19
00:00:55,843 --> 00:00:59,820
For an inductor, the inductance and
the largest current it can carry.

20
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:05,014
Tolerance tells you how far the real
value can be from the printed one.

21
00:01:05,014 --> 00:01:09,638
A one kilo ohm resistor with five
percent tolerance can be anywhere from

22
00:01:09,638 --> 00:01:13,344
nine hundred and fifty to
one thousand and fifty ohms.

23
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:17,139
Now let's test Aarav's
resistor on the breadboard.

24
00:01:17,139 --> 00:01:21,610
It is one kilo ohm, and its power
rating is a quarter of a watt.

25
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:23,369
Turn up the supply.

26
00:01:23,369 --> 00:01:27,742
The current rises, and the power
rises even faster, because it depends

27
00:01:27,742 --> 00:01:29,346
on the voltage squared.

28
00:01:29,696 --> 00:01:33,000
At ten volts, the power
is zero point one watts.

29
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,131
That is well inside the rating.

30
00:01:35,131 --> 00:01:36,798
The resistor stays cool.

31
00:01:37,148 --> 00:01:40,535
At twenty volts, the power
is zero point four watts.

32
00:01:40,535 --> 00:01:42,252
That is above the rating.

33
00:01:42,602 --> 00:01:44,084
The resistor overheats.

34
00:01:44,084 --> 00:01:46,542
That is exactly what happened to Aarav.

35
00:01:46,542 --> 00:01:49,779
The value was right, but
the rating was too small.

36
00:01:50,429 --> 00:01:52,550
The value tells you what a part is.

37
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:54,927
The rating tells you what it can survive.

38
00:01:55,727 --> 00:01:58,353
So what is the most a resistor can take?

39
00:01:58,353 --> 00:01:59,615
Let's work it out.

40
00:01:59,965 --> 00:02:02,353
Power is voltage times current.

41
00:02:02,353 --> 00:02:04,059
P equals V times I.

42
00:02:04,759 --> 00:02:07,277
From Ohm's law, I equals V by R.

43
00:02:07,277 --> 00:02:09,414
So P equals V squared by R.

44
00:02:09,414 --> 00:02:12,355
Or, in terms of current, I squared R.

45
00:02:13,054 --> 00:02:16,067
Now set P equal to the rating, and solve.

46
00:02:16,067 --> 00:02:19,982
The largest safe voltage is
the square root of P times R.

47
00:02:20,682 --> 00:02:24,690
And the largest safe current
is the square root of P by R.

48
00:02:24,690 --> 00:02:26,536
P in watts, R in ohms.

49
00:02:27,186 --> 00:02:28,391
Worked example.

50
00:02:28,391 --> 00:02:31,500
One kilo ohm, rated a quarter of a watt.

51
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:36,833
V max equals the square root of zero
point two five times one thousand.

52
00:02:36,833 --> 00:02:39,700
That is about fifteen point eight volts.

53
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,960
I max equals the square root of
zero point two five by one thousand.

54
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:47,554
About fifteen point eight milliamperes.

55
00:02:47,554 --> 00:02:49,474
Twenty volts was too much.

56
00:02:50,124 --> 00:02:51,287
One more thing.

57
00:02:51,287 --> 00:02:54,379
A rating holds only up
to a stated temperature.

58
00:02:54,729 --> 00:02:59,121
In this example datasheet, the full
quarter watt is allowed up to seventy

59
00:02:59,121 --> 00:03:00,021
degrees.

60
00:03:00,152 --> 00:03:04,622
Above that, the allowed power falls
in a straight line, down to zero at

61
00:03:04,622 --> 00:03:06,896
one hundred and fifty five degrees.

62
00:03:06,896 --> 00:03:08,524
This is called derating.

63
00:03:09,174 --> 00:03:10,640
Now, the exam trap.

64
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:15,528
Many students think the absolute
maximum rating is a safe value to work

65
00:03:15,528 --> 00:03:16,428
at.

66
00:03:16,005 --> 00:03:16,969
It is not.

67
00:03:17,319 --> 00:03:20,041
It is the limit where damage can begin.

68
00:03:20,041 --> 00:03:24,826
Always design inside the recommended
operating values, with room to spare.

69
00:03:25,476 --> 00:03:27,108
Where is this asked?

70
00:03:27,108 --> 00:03:28,740
In PUC board papers.

71
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:33,821
List the specifications of a resistor
or a capacitor, or work out a power

72
00:03:33,821 --> 00:03:35,957
rating or a tolerance range.

73
00:03:36,607 --> 00:03:37,507
Try this.

74
00:03:37,430 --> 00:03:41,462
A four hundred and seventy ohm
resistor is rated half a watt.

75
00:03:41,462 --> 00:03:44,663
What is the largest current
it can safely carry?

76
00:03:50,063 --> 00:03:50,963
C.

77
00:03:50,511 --> 00:03:53,362
About thirty two point six milliamperes.

78
00:03:53,712 --> 00:03:57,057
Use I max equals the
square root of P by R.

79
00:03:57,407 --> 00:04:01,846
That is the square root of zero point
five divided by four hundred and

80
00:04:01,846 --> 00:04:02,746
seventy.

81
00:04:02,914 --> 00:04:06,895
The square root of zero
point zero zero one zero six.

82
00:04:06,895 --> 00:04:12,809
About zero point zero three two six amperes,
or thirty two point six milliamperes.

83
00:04:13,459 --> 00:04:14,980
Screenshot this card.

84
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:22,030
That is the end of this chapter,
but the series continues.

85
00:04:22,030 --> 00:04:25,919
More PUC Electronics is coming
soon, so follow the playlist.
